My pet was lost through a trap door and searching
two levels down didn't find it, my situation was
uninteresting, I had suicide on my mind, risky
behavior is the usual technique to provoke Yet
Another Deliberate Death in NetHack, so I stuck all
my gold in my (rogue's) sack, grabbed everything in
the shop, and started reading stuff carried or
laying around to see if I could escape without
paying, since I had the level thoroughly mapped, and
diving down either to Dlvl3 or to toplevel mines
gave me two possible escapes in the desired
direction.

First was a spellbook; two reading failures didn't
get me wrenched away. The third try memorized a
spell of Dig.

You cad, this isn't a free reading library!
You owe the shopkeeper 533 zorkmids.

That spell would be a great way to get out of the
shop if I could immobilize the shopkeeper, and if I
could use it at all, but a level 5 spell is not too
useful to an XL 1 rogue.

Well, life is still pretty valueless, what other
reading material can I try?

Next was a scroll of genocide! Whee, time to take
out my most disliked monster:

genocide which monster: rust monster

Calling in some rust monsters.

Oops. About reading un-BUC_IDd genocide scrolls: bad
idea in general and specifically right now.

There went the plate mail I was trying to smuggle
out of the shop without purchasing: thoroughly
rusted before I got another turn, so I took it off
while the rust monsters flailed away harmlessly, but
brilliantly forgot to drop it, so stayed burdened.

Hmm, several more unIDd scrolls, and the urge to
suicide has only increased, let's try another one:

Thinking of Maud, you forget everything else.

So much for knowing which way to run if one of
these is teleport.

That didn't help much. Next scroll!

The scroll disappears. Call it: Disappeared.  Hmm,
why are these three rust monsters next to me now in
inverse video?

The rust monster touches the rust monster.
The rust monster touches the rust monster. 
The rust monster touches the rust monster.
The rust monster misses the rust monster. 
The rust monster misses the rust monster.
The rust monster misses the rust monster. 
The rust monster misses the rust monster.
The rust monster touches the rust monster. 

Wonderful. I now have three rust monster pets (the
three in a vertical line next to me), doing zero
damage per attack, attacking three hostile rust
monsters, also doing zero damage per attack. Could
be a long, long fight, before I starve to death.

They aren't going to be much help against the
shopkeeper, either, except as roadblocks.

Trying to survive long enough to get to an XL
where I can cast dig is way tedious looking.

Nothing to do but read more scrolls.

As you read the scroll, it disappears.
You feel very greedy, and sense gold!

Flipping useful, lots of little yellow dollar signs
in parts of the level I'll probably never see in
this PC's lifetime.

Two scrolls left.

As you read the scroll, it disappears.
You have a strange feeling for a moment, then it
passes.

Call a scroll labeled KERNOD WEL: Nothing Happened

[The rust monsters miss each other, ten times.]

Last chance!

As you read the scroll, it disappears.
A lit field surrounds you.

And that, ladies and gentlemen, is why I've
never ascended at NetHack in 17+ years of trying.

xanthian, headed over to kick a shopkeeper in the
shins, if I can just waste these hostile rust
monsters to have room enough to move at all.

But wait,

[Oh, piffle, this is turning into a game diary, when
it was originally intended as a mere anecdotal
whine.]

This un-payed-for ring I put on, unIDed, as a
possible means of escape, made me shudder for a
moment -- a quick source dive tells me I'm either
trying to teleport, or polymorph. Best take off the
armor, which is useless anyway, and see if
"teleport" is the right answer; if not, polymorphing
might wreck my only remaining (leather) armor,
something the rust monsters cannot affect, and, now
that I'm closer to the doorway, the shopkeeper isn't
going to let anything in from the hallway anyway.
Two rust monsters kicked to death now, one to go.
Too bad the ring is cursed, though.

Suicide: it isn't as easy as it looks on TV.

Many, many turns later, the last rust monster enemy
killed, two rust monster corpses _eaten_, lots of
resting waiting for any action from the RNG and ...
suddenly I'm an overloaded vixen (not a run-away
shoplifter, as hoped), and, conceptually, thereby
protected by the anti-FoxHunting patch I suppose.

[Did anyone stop to consider the patch's effects on
a PC who _was_ a fox? Seems like at a minimum, if
you're attacked by monsters _as_ a fox, the Kops,
tame, should be called in to protect you!]

Hmm. Much later still, my cursed ring of polymorph
turns me into a purple worm, and shopkeeper
Hebiwerie dies as worm food, a common enough fate,
dropping the usual shopkeeper goodies. It's too bad
I don't have polymorph control, wandering around the
dungeon as an all-devouring annelid looks promising
as heck.

It's even more too bad my stinking pet rust monsters
Rusty, Oxidize, and Corrode ate every bit of iron in
my inventory when I foolishly stepped away from
where, as an overburdened vixen, I had dropped it.
Goodbye, daggers, helm, swords! I'm no longer in the
protection racket game, having ratcheted up to XL 5
in the midst of trying to stay alive. A shame, that
is, since Hebiwerie was carrying enough loot to make
Mack the Knife slaver.

I _really_ need a magic whistle and a polymorph trap
to turn these guys' empty minds to other interests.
Now where did I leave that Mines entrance, anyway?
Oh, that's right -- with Maud for safekeeping.

Also, somewhere in all the futile messages about
rust monsters getting in closer touch with those
around them, I got a sad feeling about Barks A Lot
I. It must be that he got teleported into a closet
or a vault rather than falling through a trap door
as I had surmised.

Suddenly, from being bored and trying fruitlessly to
suicide, I'm back to being intensely interested in
this game, and thus very close to YASDing merely by
attitude alone.

Should I also gripe that my no-polyself conduct is
"Gone with the Wind"? Probably not, it would be a
low-rent thing to do.  Suicide-wannabies pretty much
lose all "whine rights" about just how they achieve
their demise and along what pathways.

